There have been consistent efforts put in place by the SRH Working Group and partners to capture and 
review maternal and perinatal deaths across the Rohingya response and subsequent identification of 
ways to reduce preventable maternal and perinatal deaths. However, the number of maternal and 
perinatal deaths occurring across the response is still unacceptably high with an average of 1 maternal 
death and 15 perinatal deaths occurring per week. 
In previous years, the emphasis of the Maternal and Perinatal Mortality Surveillance and Response 
(MPMSR) system was developed and strengthened for maternal deaths only. However, starting in August 
2023, the surveillance and response system for perinatal deaths was established. The number of 
perinatal deaths reported in this annual report only reflects the perinatal deaths that occurred between 
August and December 2023. 
The Maternal and Perinatal Surveillance and Death Review Report is a comprehensive analysis aimed at 
understanding and addressing the factors contributing to maternal and perinatal mortality. This report 
presents critical data, identifies systemic gaps, and proposes strategic interventions to enhance the 
health outcomes for mothers and newborns. 
In 2023, there were 57 maternal deaths reported of which 43 (75%) occurred at health facilities or 
during referral from one health facility to another while 14 (25%) occurred at community level.In 
addition and like the previous years, the report highlights a range of direct and indirect causes of 
maternal deaths, including obstetric hemorrhage (35%; n = 20) and hypertensive disorder (33%; n = 19) 
as well as socio-economic and healthcare access issues that exacerbate these conditions. 
