In response to the massive influx of Rohingya refugees in Bangladesh in 2017, efforts to manage this large scale humanitarian emergency have been established, including the development of a maternal mortality surveillance system. The first cornerstone was the “Population and Demographic survey” which was conducted by ICDDRB with support from UNFPA to know the demographic profile of the Rohingya population in need in Cox’s Bazar which was followed by a situational analysis of “Maternal and Perinatal Mortality Surveillance” by UNFPA in association with CDC, Atlanta. Measurement of maternal mortality among the Rohingya refugee population has progressed over time starting with a retrospective Reproductive Age Mortality Study (RAMOS) from August 2017 to August 2018. The RAMOS report (2019) by CDC Atlanta and UNFPA informed and initiated the development of the Maternal and Perinatal Mortality Surveillance and Response (MPMSR) in the Rohingya refugee response. In March 2019, WHO EWARS was introduced for notification of the mortalities in the purview of community-based surveillance (CBS). A multi-agency coordination mechanism was established under the SRH working group as MPMSR committee to introduce the prospective surveillance in the Rohingya response. The surveillance captures all the deaths during pregnancy, childbirth, and puerperium reported and reviews, irrespective of the place of death i.e., at home, in the facility, in transit, or anywhere in the community. The mortality review process is done at community level and facility level. At the community level, all Women of reproductive age deaths are reviewed by verbal autopsy method. Followed by the notifications, facility-based maternal death audits are arranged and conducted for each death during pregnancy, childbirth,…

