Maternal death is defined as the death of a woman while pregnant or within 42 days of termination of pregnancy, irrespective of the duration and the site of the pregnancy, from any cause related to or aggravated by the pregnancy or its management, but not from accidental or incidental causes (9,12). A total of 57 (18%) WRA of reproductive health age deaths were reclassified as maternal deaths which occurred within 42 days after delivery and is a decrease from that reported in 2022. Among the maternal deaths, 43 (75%) occurred at health facilities or during referral from one health facility to another, while 14 (25%) occurred at home or before admission to a primary health facility. The proportion of facility-based deliveries increased compared to those reported in 2022. Twenty-two (71%) of the maternal deaths had been delivered at a health facility while 29% were delivered at home. Twenty-nine (51%) of the mothers that died had received at least 4 ANC visits which is a decrease from the 42% reported last year. Of the maternal deaths reported, 5 (9%) were adolescents aged 15-19 years which is lower than the proportion of adolescent maternal deaths reported in 2022, 40 (70%) were aged 20-34 years while 12 (21%) were aged 35-44 years. Of all the maternal deaths, 11 (19%) also had stillbirths which is lower than the proportion reported in 2022. Moreover, this was the first pregnancy for 11 (19%) of all the mothers who died (primigravida). 13 (22%) of all the maternal deaths had been delivered by cesarean section in 2023 compared to 12 (20%) in 2022. The average monthly maternal death was 5, as Figure 2 below shows.

